lwip, vendored for the byedpid utun layer, copied from senko's copy

this tree is senko's copy (daemon/third_party/lwip at senko commit f79181f)
without src/Filelists.mk, src/FILES, CHANGELOG, README and UPGRADING. the
sources in src/core and src/include are the upstream files. the paths below
name senko's tree, here they are daemon/tun/third_party/lwip.

upstream:     https://git.savannah.nongnu.org/git/lwip.git
release:      lwIP 2.2.1
tag:          STABLE-2_2_1_RELEASE
commit:       77dcd25a72509eb83f72b033d219b1d40cd8eb95
archive:      https://download.savannah.nongnu.org/releases/lwip/lwip-2.2.1.zip
archive sha256: 7b622662dba2383d71f874f2e494b54ae948531559c17acbe75797966d646878
imported:     2026-09-22
license:      BSD 3-clause, see COPYING. compatible with senko's gplv2

the files here are copied from the tag above, unmodified. the published zip
and the tag hold the same sources, they differ only in line endings (the zip
ships crlf), so the import was taken from the tag to keep the tree free of
carriage returns. to verify:

  git clone --depth 1 --branch STABLE-2_2_1_RELEASE \
      https://git.savannah.nongnu.org/git/lwip.git
  diff -r lwip/src/core       daemon/third_party/lwip/src/core
  diff -r lwip/src/include    daemon/third_party/lwip/src/include

what was imported:

  src/core            the stack itself, ipv4 and ipv6 included
  src/include/lwip    public and private headers
  src/include/netif   headers the core includes when a feature is enabled
  src/Filelists.mk    upstream's own file list, kept as the reference for
                      which sources belong to which feature
  src/FILES, COPYING, CHANGELOG, README, UPGRADING

what was left out, because senko drives the stack through the raw api from a
single thread and never uses the parts that need an os or a socket layer:

  src/api             netconn and socket apis
  src/apps            http, mqtt, sntp and the rest
  src/netif/*.c       ethernet, ppp, slip, 6lowpan and bridge drivers.
                      the utun device is the only interface senko attaches
  src/include/compat  posix socket compatibility headers

senko's configuration lives outside this directory, so nothing here has to be
edited to build it. this directory is never modified locally: a fix belongs
upstream or in senko's own adapter, and the next version is imported the same
way this one was.

nothing is downloaded during a build.
